    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d860">

                <pb facs="0899" n="885" sameAs="frus:frus1944v07/pg885"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">818.51/8–3044</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chargé in Costa Rica</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Trueblood</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 645</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-08-30">August 30,
                        1944</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The Secretary of State encloses, at the request of the Export-Import Bank, for
                    transmission to the Costa Rican Minister of Finance<anchor n="8"
                        xml:id="d860-f1"/> a letter addressed to him on August 23, 1944, by the
                    President of the Bank.<anchor n="9" xml:id="d860-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>The particular attention of the Embassy is directed to the last paragraph of the
                    Bank’s letter stating that: “In view of the foregoing as well as the intention
                    of your Government to buttress President Picado’s fiscal policies by the
                    adoption of various reforms recommended by Messrs. Ness and Kekich,<anchor
                        n="10" xml:id="d860-f3"/> we believe, Mr. Minister, that you will concur in
                    the opinion that consideration of an amendment of our Agreement of July 9,
                        1942<anchor n="11" xml:id="d860-f4"/> should be postponed.” The Bank refers
                    to a number of recommendations for the improvement and strengthening of the
                    methods of handling Costa Rican finances suggested by Messrs. Ness and Kekich of
                    the United States Treasury Department who, it will be recalled, made studies of
                    the Costa Rican fiscal situation in 1942 and 1943 at the request of that
                    Government.</p>
                <p>For the Embassy’s confidential information, during the recent discussions in
                    Washington Señor Peña<anchor n="12" xml:id="d860-f5"/> suggested that if the
                        Bank<anchor n="13" xml:id="d860-f6"/> agreed to the proposed amendment to
                    the 1942 agreement, it should do so only on condition that the Costa Rican
                    Government adopt the fiscal reforms which it had said it intended to make.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d860-f1" n="8" xml:id="d860-f1-content">
                <p>Alvaro Bonilla Lara.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d860-f2" n="9" xml:id="d860-f2-content">
                <p>Warren Lee Pierson; letter not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d860-f3" n="10" xml:id="d860-f3-content">
                <p>Thomas Kekich, Bureau of Accounts, Treasury Department.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d860-f4" n="11" xml:id="d860-f4-content">
                <p>This loan agreement, not printed, was drafted in accordance with terms described
                    in Department’s telegram 264, June 27, 1942, 3 p.m., to San José, <ref
                        target="frus1942v06#pg_251">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1942, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">vi</hi>, p. 251</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d860-f5" n="12" xml:id="d860-f5-content">
                <p>Julio Peña Morua, General Manager of the National Bank of Costa Rica.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d860-f6" n="13" xml:id="d860-f6-content">
                <p>Presumably the Export-Import Bank of Washington.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
